  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nLow pressure will continue to slowly move east across the area\nthrough tonight. As of now, the most widespread showers with\nheavy rainfall rates are over Lake Erie, but showers and\nthunderstorms will most likely begin to fill in as the upper\ntrough axis moves into a more unstable environment across the\neastern half of the area this afternoon. Clearing has allowed \nMLCAPE values to increase to around 1000 J/kg across portions of\nNE OH/NW PA and at least 500 J/kg elsewhere along/east of I-71.\nThis will be the area to watch for thunderstorm and heavy rain \ndevelopment this afternoon into this evening. PWAT values are \ncurrently around 1.5 to 1.75 inches and dew points are in the \nupper 70s and lower 70s, which supports potential for torrential\nrainfall rates. There is also potential for training in \nlocations where mean steering flow is more southerly. The \ntorrential rainfall rates in tandem with training could result \nin flash flooding. The best chance of heavy rainfall will be \nwhere showers are more convective and at this point that appears\nto be most favorable across portions of NE OH/NW PA. A Flood \nWatch remains in effect for most western zones until 5 PM and \nalong/east of I-71 until midnight tonight. The heaviest rainfall\nhas ended for Lucas/Wood/Hancock counties so have been able to \ncancel that portion of the watch.\n\nGiven the destabilization in the eastern half of the area and\neffective bulk shear values of around 35 knots, there is\npotential for damaging wind gusts in any robust thunderstorms\nthat develop. There is also a low-end tornado risk given the\nwind field and low LCLs. There is a Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5)\nof severe weather across the eastern half of the area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe low will exit to the east on Monday, allowing surface high \npressure to expand across the area as an upper low settles to\nthe south of the region through Tuesday. Dry weather is \nexpected for the first two days of the week before the upper\nlow lifts north in response to a trough moving east across the\nUpper Midwest. This will cause shower and thunderstorm chances \nto return to the area Wednesday into next weekend. Temperatures\nwill generally be around normal next week with highs in the 80s\neach day and lows in the 60s most nights. Monday night will be\nthe chilliest night of the week with widespread highs in the 50s\nexpected.",
